Author: piggidragon <piggidragon.dev@proton.me>
Date:   Mon Aug 24 21:26:13 2026 +0200

    cuda: measure the achievable copy/compute overlap for host KV
    
    With the store side eliminated as a suspect, the decode token is copy plus
    compute in series and the only lever left is running them concurrently. The
    ceiling is max(copy, compute) instead of copy + compute. Both terms are
    measurable: the device-resident arm gives compute, and the difference against
    the host-resident arm gives copy.
    
      depth   host KV  ms/tok   device KV  ms/tok   copy     perfect overlap
      4,096   31.72    31.52    39.0152    25.63    5.89 ms  39.02 t/s (+23.0%)
      16,384  19.69    50.78    36.1072    27.70    23.08 ms 36.11 t/s (+83.4%)
      32,768  13.03    76.77    32.5832    30.69    46.08 ms 21.71 t/s (+66.6%)
    
    The implied copy at 32,768 is 46.08 ms against 47.02 ms of blocking
    host-to-device from the telemetry, so the no-overlap model holds to within 2%.
    
    GGML_KV_OVERLAP_PROBE issues the pinned-host delivery on a dedicated
    non-blocking copy stream and returns true without recording an event the
    compute stream ever waits on. Same bytes, same order; the attention kernels
    simply do not wait and read what the destination held from the previous token.
    Output is incorrect by construction. The arm measures exactly one thing: how
    much of the copy the copy engine retires underneath the kernels.
    
    A/B/A/B:
    
      depth  4,096, r=5: 31.7287/31.7165 -> 38.4860/38.5025  (+21.3%, 98.6% of device)
      depth 16,384, r=3: 19.7025/19.6820 -> 34.8800/34.8439  (+77.0%, 96.5% of device)
      depth 32,768, r=3: 13.0142         -> 20.8258          (+60.0%, 95.9% of ceiling)
    
    The copy engine on this device overlaps host-to-device transfer with kernel
    execution almost perfectly. With the ordering gone, host-resident decode
    reaches 96-99% of its own predicted ceiling at every depth. The entire host-KV
    decode penalty is the ordering, not the bandwidth -- transfers already run at
    the practical PCIe 4.0 x16 rate.
    
    This also corrects Experiment 026, which rejected prefetching partly because
    "the host blocks for essentially the whole token, so there is no window to
    prefetch into." The ceiling probe shows removing the host blocking is worth
    0.18%, and this probe shows the window hides 96-99% of the copy. That +1.38%
    was an implementation that did not collect the overlap available to it.
    
    The probe is not a candidate implementation and cannot become one by adding a
    wait: a correct version issues split k+1's delivery while split k's kernels
    run, which needs staging buffers ggml-alloc cannot recycle underneath an
    in-flight copy. Reverted with the other measurement-only changes.
    
